    ('c4d4863e-2344-49aa-8699-457d71985c96'::uuid, 'period_name', '1500 - 500 BC', '3000 - 1500 BC', 'fields-wd5-2026-10-25p-s008:c4d4863e-2344-49aa-8699-457d71985c96:period_name', 'fields-wd5-2026-10-25p-s008 (wd5-derive-period-name): period_start -2686 (written in this step)', '[{"source": "pipeline/utils/text.py:categorize_period", "url": "pipeline/utils/text.py", "quote": "categorize_period(-2686) = ''3000 - 1500 BC''"}, {"source": "output/remediation/gold_standard/GOLD_STANDARD.md:79", "url": "output/remediation/gold_standard/GOLD_STANDARD.md", "quote": "| `period_name` | equals `categorize_period(period_start)` |"}, {"source": "WD5 decision (counted, round 0, field_researcher:wd5-r0-b0012)", "decision": "replace", "status": "MISSING", "reasoning": "The page reproduces the excavator Zahi Hawass''s report ''The Discovery of the Osiris Shaft at Giza'' (it names that report as its source) and concludes that the shaft was first cut in the Old Kingdom (it ties the construction to the 6th Dynasty, from red polished pottery of that date) and reused in the New Kingdom and the 26th Dynasty. Selim Hassan''s 1933-34 date was Saitic, and Hawass''s earlier web statement gave the New Kingdom, 1550 BC, but his published conclusion puts the start of the shaft in the Old Kingdom, which lies in a different bucket than the stored rule-made -800.", "value_page": null, "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5 (Claude Code agent)", "adversarial": {"answered_by": "adversarial:adv-r1-b0002", "model": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5 (Claude Code agent)", "reason": "Hawass''s published conclusion is ''first cut in the Old Kingdom'', but the report narrows it from 6th Dynasty sherds only (''This suggests''), Selim Hassan dated the shaft Saite and Hawass elsewhere gave the New Kingdom, so the sources disagree and -2686 (start of the Old Kingdom) is not a start year for the shaft.", "round": 1, "verdict": "unclear"}, "owner": {"at": "2026-10-25T07:49:50+00:00", "decision": "D69", "reason": "owne